# The operator seam, which is the largest of elixir's three and the reason the
# caesura row exists.
#
# `scan_newline` marks its end immediately after the newline and its inline
# whitespace, then only looks at what follows to decide *which* terminal it is:
# a comment, a `do`, or a binary operator. Three names, one function, no memory
# - and the claim is the opposite of an automatic semicolon's. ecma's seam says
# a statement ENDED here; elixir's says it did NOT, and the next line resumes
# it. That is why this row has no `body`: a break followed by none of the three
# is not a token at all, the extras eat it, and nothing is owed.
#
# These bytes are the falsifier for the operator arm specifically. Without it
# `x =` ends at the newline and `a` and `|> f()` become their own roots; with it
# the whole thing is one right-nested `binary_operator`, which is what
# tree-sitter builds and what these claims are taken from.
roots 1
mends 0
holds binary_operator
holds call
lacks ERROR
spans binary_operator 0 16
spans call 13 16
